\section{Where Each Setting Applies}{

Each argument takes an object built by a specific control function. Passing
an object from another one stops with an error that names the right
function.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Estimation in \code{\link[=netest]{netest()}}:}
\itemize{
\item \code{set.control.ergm} takes \code{\link[ergm:control.ergm]{ergm::control.ergm()}}, for the ERGM fit with
the edges dissolution approximation (\code{edapprox = TRUE}).
\item \code{set.control.ergm.ego} takes \code{ergm.ego::control.ergm.ego()}, for the
same fit when \code{nw} is an \code{egor} object.
\item \code{set.control.tergm} takes \code{\link[tergm:control.tergm]{tergm::control.tergm()}}, for the full STERGM
fit (\code{edapprox = FALSE}).
}
\item \strong{Simulation in \code{\link[=netdx]{netdx()}} and \code{\link[=control.net]{control.net()}}:}
\itemize{
\item \code{set.control.ergm} takes \code{\link[ergm:control.simulate.formula]{ergm::control.simulate.formula()}}, for the
cross-sectional network that the dynamic simulation starts from, and
for the static diagnostics of \code{netdx(dynamic = FALSE)}. It is used only
for fits with \code{edapprox = TRUE}; a full STERGM fit starts from the
network saved by \code{\link[=netest]{netest()}}.
\item \code{set.control.tergm} takes \code{\link[tergm:control.simulate.formula.tergm]{tergm::control.simulate.formula.tergm()}},
for each time step of the dynamic network simulation, both in the
diagnostics and in the epidemic model.
}
}

In a model with several network layers, \code{\link[=control.net]{control.net()}} takes one control
object per layer through \code{\link[=multilayer]{multilayer()}}.
}

\section{Defaults Set by EpiModel}{

Three defaults differ from those of the control functions themselves:
\itemize{
\item \code{set.control.tergm} in \code{\link[=netest]{netest()}} is
\code{control.tergm(MCMC.maxchanges = .Machine$integer.max)}.
\item \code{set.control.tergm} in \code{\link[=netdx]{netdx()}} and \code{\link[=control.net]{control.net()}} is
\code{control.simulate.formula.tergm(MCMC.maxchanges = .Machine$integer.max)}.
This raises the number of network changes that \code{tergm} can record in one
simulation above its default limit of 1e6, which a long \code{\link[=netdx]{netdx()}} run
can exceed.
\item \code{set.control.ergm} in \code{\link[=control.net]{control.net()}} is
\code{control.simulate.formula(MCMC.burnin = 2e5)}, a longer burn-in for the
starting network than the \code{ergm} default.
}

A control object passed to one of these arguments replaces the default as a
whole, not only the settings it names, so restate these settings alongside
any others (see the examples).
}

\section{Which Stage to Adjust}{

An error or a stalled fit in \code{\link[=netest]{netest()}} calls for the estimation settings
below. When the fit succeeds but the dynamic diagnostics of \code{\link[=netdx]{netdx()}} miss
the formation targets, the cause can lie in the simulation, in the edges
dissolution approximation, or in the cross-sectional fit:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{The per-step burn-in of the simulation.} By default, \code{tergm} sets
the number of proposals in each time step adaptively. With short
partnership durations it can end a time step before the network has
replaced the partnerships that dissolved, so statistics such as the edge
count settle below their targets. This applies to fits with and without
the approximation. Setting \code{MCMC.burnin.min} and \code{MCMC.burnin.max} in
\code{set.control.tergm} to the same value (such as 1e4) fixes the number of
proposals per time step and is quick to try.
\item \strong{The edges dissolution approximation.} For fits with
\code{edapprox = TRUE}, run static diagnostics with \code{netdx(dynamic = FALSE)}.
If they are on target but the dynamic diagnostics remain off with a
fixed per-step burn-in, the approximation is the likely cause. It loses
accuracy when many partnerships form and dissolve at each time step,
that is, when durations are short or density is high. Fit the full
STERGM with \code{edapprox = FALSE}, or redefine the time step as a shorter
unit so that durations span more time steps, rescaling every other
time-based parameter of the model to match.
\item \strong{The cross-sectional fit.} If the static diagnostics are also off, the
ERGM fit has not reached the targets, or the static simulation has not
moved far enough from its starting network. On a large network, raise
\code{MCMC.burnin} and \code{MCMC.interval} in the \code{set.control.ergm} of \code{\link[=netdx]{netdx()}}
before changing the estimation settings of \code{\link[=netest]{netest()}}.
}

Read the \verb{Pct Diff} column of \code{\link[=print.netdx]{print.netdx()}} alongside the \verb{Z Score}. The
standard error behind the Z score shrinks with the effective sample size, so
many static draws can make a difference of a fraction of a percent
statistically significant, while the autocorrelation between time steps
keeps the effective sample size of a dynamic simulation, and so its Z
scores, smaller.

The simulation settings that bring the \code{\link[=netdx]{netdx()}} diagnostics on target
belong in \code{\link[=control.net]{control.net()}} as well: the epidemic simulation steps the network
forward with the same \code{tergm} simulation, so without them it runs on the
network that the diagnostics flagged.
}

\section{Settings Most Often Changed}{

The control functions have many arguments, of which a few account for most
adjustments in practice. Their help pages document the rest.

For the ERGM fit, in \code{\link[ergm:control.ergm]{ergm::control.ergm()}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{MCMLE.maxit}: the maximum number of MCMLE iterations. Raise it for a fit
that is still moving toward the targets when it stops.
\item \code{MCMC.samplesize}, \code{MCMC.interval}, and \code{MCMC.burnin}: the size,
spacing, and burn-in of the MCMC sample drawn at each iteration. Larger
networks generally need a longer interval and burn-in. By default \code{ergm}
extends the sample until it reaches the effective size set by
\code{MCMLE.effectiveSize}; setting \code{MCMLE.effectiveSize = NULL} holds it at
\code{MCMC.samplesize} draws, which bounds the time per iteration.
\item \code{SAN.nsteps.times} and \code{SAN.maxit}: simulated annealing (SAN) builds the
starting network that matches the target statistics. \code{SAN.nsteps.times}
scales the total number of SAN proposals, and \code{SAN.maxit} divides them
into that many runs. Raise \code{SAN.nsteps.times} when SAN does not reach the
targets.
\item \code{MCMC.prop}: hints for the MCMC proposal (see \link[ergm:ergmHint]{ergm::ergmHint}). When
most ties fall within a few cells of a mixing matrix, a stratified
proposal such as \code{~strat(attr = ~age.grp) + sparse} stops the sampler
from spending its proposals on cells that hold few or no ties. Listing
any hint replaces the default ones, so list \code{sparse} as well.
\item \code{MCMC.maxedges}: the maximum number of edges in a sampled network,
unlimited by default. A finite value makes a fit that wanders into dense,
degenerate networks stop with an error rather than run for hours.
\item \code{MCMLE.steplength} with \code{MCMLE.steplength.margin = NULL}: a fixed step
length below 1 (such as 0.5) keeps the first iterations of a hard fit
from overshooting. With the default margin, \code{MCMLE.steplength} is only
the maximum step length.
\item \code{parallel}: the number of cores across which the MCMC sample is drawn.
}

For the full STERGM fit, in \code{\link[tergm:control.tergm]{tergm::control.tergm()}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{EGMME.MCMC.burnin.min} and \code{EGMME.MCMC.burnin.max}: the number of
proposals per time step during estimation, set adaptively by default.
Setting both to the same value fixes it, which can stabilize a fit that
otherwise fails. In the course examples, 1e4 works for a network of
1,000 nodes, and larger networks may need a value on the order of the
network size. Fix the per-step burn-in of the simulation to the same
value.
\item \code{SAN.nsteps.times}, \code{SAN.maxit}, and \code{MCMC.prop} act as in
\code{\link[ergm:control.ergm]{ergm::control.ergm()}}.
}

For the dynamic simulation, in \code{\link[tergm:control.simulate.formula.tergm]{tergm::control.simulate.formula.tergm()}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{MCMC.burnin.min} and \code{MCMC.burnin.max}: the number of proposals per
time step. Setting both to the same value fixes it, as described above.
\code{tergm} has no \code{MCMC.interval}; this pair takes its place.
\item \code{MCMC.maxchanges}: restate \code{.Machine$integer.max} when passing any other
setting.
\item \code{MCMC.prop}: the default, \code{~discord + sparse}, proposes a share of its
toggles (set by \code{MCMC.prop.args = list(discordance_fraction = )}, 0.5 by
default) on dyads that have already changed within the time step. For
strongly structured mixing, add a \code{strat()} hint as in estimation and
keep \code{discord} and \code{sparse} in the formula.
}

For the cross-sectional simulation, in \code{\link[ergm:control.simulate.formula]{ergm::control.simulate.formula()}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{MCMC.burnin} and \code{MCMC.interval}: the burn-in before the starting
network (or the first static draw) and the spacing between static draws.
}
}

\examples{
\donttest{
nw <- network_initialize(n = 100)
formation <- ~edges + concurrent
target.stats <- c(50, 20)
coef.diss <- dissolution_coefs(dissolution = ~offset(edges), duration = 10)
est <- netest(nw, formation, target.stats, coef.diss)

# Fix the number of proposals per time step in the dynamic simulation,
# restating the MCMC.maxchanges default that EpiModel sets
sim.control <- control.simulate.formula.tergm(
  MCMC.burnin.min = 1e4,
  MCMC.burnin.max = 1e4,
  MCMC.maxchanges = .Machine$integer.max
)
dx <- netdx(est, nsims = 2, nsteps = 100, set.control.tergm = sim.control,
            verbose = FALSE)
dx

# Pass the same settings to the epidemic simulation
param <- param.net(inf.prob = 0.3, act.rate = 1)
init <- init.net(i.num = 10)
control <- control.net(type = "SI", nsims = 1, nsteps = 50,
                       set.control.tergm = sim.control, verbose = FALSE)
sim <- netsim(est, param, init, control)
}

\dontrun{
# Estimation settings for a larger or slower ERGM fit
est <- netest(nw, formation, target.stats, coef.diss,
              set.control.ergm = control.ergm(MCMLE.maxit = 500,
                                              MCMC.samplesize = 1e4,
                                              MCMC.interval = 5e3,
                                              SAN.nsteps.times = 16,
                                              parallel = 4))

# Full STERGM fit with a fixed number of proposals per time step
est <- netest(nw, formation, target.stats, coef.diss, edapprox = FALSE,
              set.control.tergm = control.tergm(
                EGMME.MCMC.burnin.min = 1e4,
                EGMME.MCMC.burnin.max = 1e4,
                MCMC.maxchanges = .Machine$integer.max
              ))
dx <- netdx(est, nsims = 5, nsteps = 500, set.control.tergm = sim.control)
}

}
